{"is_error":false,"duration_api_ms":78268,"num_turns":2,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"d96d8832-c27f-5a30-96b6-20cb8541ae8f","total_cost_usd":0.556503,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9497,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":7245,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9497,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7245,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9497,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":9497},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":4203,"outputTokens":18,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.004293,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty"},"claude-fable-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":7245,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":9497,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.55221,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"canonicalModel":"claude-fable-5","provider":"firstParty"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"reviewer_participant_id\":\"actor:dependence-free-batch-j2-reviewer-fable-46\",\"reviews\":[{\"case_id\":\"case:56e4106c5ef7a44d29c8\",\"verdict\":\"demonstrated_issue\",\"issue_class\":\"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure\",\"bounded_statement\":\"Within the supplied files for case:56e4106c5ef7a44d29c8, the selected result (two-sided Fisher exact test on 16 chamber deployments, odds ratio 21.00, p = 0.0406) is produced by entering all 16 deployment rows into a 2x2 Fisher exact test as independent trials, even though the data description designates collar_id as the independent unit and each of the 6 collars contributes 2-3 repeated deployment rows. The Fisher exact test assumes each counted trial is independent, so the repeated entries from the same collar inflate the effective sample size from 6 collars to 16 rows; a collar-level 3-vs-3 comparison cannot reach p < 0.05 under any outcome (minimum two-sided Fisher p = 0.1 for complete separation), so the reported significance depends entirely on the repeated per-collar entries.\",\"root_cause\":\"The workflow's split_counts function pools every CSV row by drainage_status into the 2x2 contingency table without any aggregation to the collar_id level, and compose() feeds those pooled row counts directly into scipy.stats.fisher_exact. Because drainage_status is a fixed property of each collar and each collar appears in 2-3 rows (repeated survey weeks), the same independent unit enters the row-independent Fisher exact procedure multiple times, which the report then explicitly frames as 16 independent trials.\",\"evidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The data description declares collar_id as the independent-unit column while a row is a single chamber closure on a collar in one survey week, establishing that multiple rows per collar are repeated measurements of one unit.\",\"observed_value\":\"Independent unit column: collar_id; one row = one closure on one collar in one survey week\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":26,\"end_line\":28,\"quoted_text\":\"One row is: one static-chamber closure on one permanent collar in one survey week\\nIndependent unit column: collar_id\\nOne trial is: one row\"}]},{\"description\":\"The data description states that each collar was revisited across survey weeks 24, 27 and 31, so every collar contributes 2 or 3 rows, and that the same collar appears in several rows of the file.\",\"observed_value\":\"6 collars, each with 2-3 deployments (16 rows total)\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":7,\"end_line\":9,\"quoted_text\":\"Each collar was revisited in survey weeks 24, 27 and 31; two collars were not\\nreachable in week 31, so those collars contribute two deployments instead of\\nthree.\"},{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":14,\"end_line\":16,\"quoted_text\":\"- `collar_id`: the permanent collar the chamber was seated on (FEN-* collars\\n  are intact, DRN-* collars are drained). The same collar appears in several\\n  rows of the file.\"}]},{\"description\":\"drainage_status, the grouping factor of the 2x2 test, is a fixed property of the collar's block rather than of the individual visit, so repeated rows from one collar always re-enter the same treatment cell.\",\"observed_value\":\"drainage_status constant within collar\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":17,\"end_line\":18,\"quoted_text\":\"- `drainage_status`: `intact` or `drained`, a property of the collar's block\\n  rather than of the individual visit.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The input data confirm repeated rows per collar: e.g. FEN-01 appears in rows D001, D007 and D013 (weeks 24, 27, 31), and DRN-01 appears in rows D004, D010 and D015.\",\"observed_value\":\"FEN-01: 3 rows; DRN-01: 3 rows; 16 rows across 6 collars\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":2,\"end_line\":2,\"quoted_text\":\"D001,FEN-01,intact,24,13.8,-3.5,yes\"},{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":8,\"end_line\":8,\"quoted_text\":\"D007,FEN-01,intact,27,17.4,-5.8,yes\"},{\"path\":\"data/input.csv\",\"start_line\":14,\"end_line\":14,\"quoted_text\":\"D013,FEN-01,intact,31,14.6,-4.7,yes\"}]},{\"description\":\"The workflow pools all rows (not collars) into the 2x2 table via split_counts and submits the row-level counts directly to scipy.stats.fisher_exact, with no per-collar aggregation feeding the test (per_collar is used only for the descriptive table).\",\"observed_value\":\"fisher_exact([[hit_i, miss_i], [hit_d, miss_d]]) on row-level counts 7/1 vs 2/6\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":21,\"end_line\":24,\"quoted_text\":\"def split_counts(rows, status):\\n    subset = [row for row in rows if row[\\\"drainage_status\\\"] == status]\\n    hits = sum(1 for row in subset if row[\\\"ebullition_detected\\\"] == DETECTED)\\n    return hits, len(subset) - hits\"},{\"path\":\"workflow/analysis.py\",\"start_line\":42,\"end_line\":48,\"quoted_text\":\"hit_i, miss_i = split_counts(rows, \\\"intact\\\")\\n    hit_d, miss_d = split_counts(rows, \\\"drained\\\")\\n    n_i = hit_i + miss_i\\n    n_d = hit_d + miss_d\\n    n_total = n_i + n_d\\n    _, p_value = fisher_exact([[hit_i, miss_i], [hit_d, miss_d]],\\n                              alternative=\\\"two-sided\\\")\"}]},{\"description\":\"The report explicitly asserts that every deployment row entered the Fisher test as one independent trial and that the test counts 16 rows produced by only 6 collars, which is the repeated entry of independent units into a row-independent procedure.\",\"observed_value\":\"16 rows treated as independent trials from 6 collars\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":22,\"end_line\":24,\"quoted_text\":\"The 16 deployments were cross-tabulated by drainage status and ebullition\\noutcome, and the 2x2 table was submitted to a two-sided Fisher exact test.\\nEvery deployment row entered the table as one independent trial.\"},{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":38,\"end_line\":39,\"quoted_text\":\"Deployments were treated as independent trials, so the test counts 16 rows\\nacross the 6 collars that produced them.\"}]},{\"description\":\"The selected statistical conclusion rests on the row-level test: p = 0.0406 is below 0.05 only because n = 16 deployments rather than 6 collars.\",\"observed_value\":\"p = 0.0406, odds ratio 21.00, 7/8 vs 2/8 deployments\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":36,\"end_line\":36,\"quoted_text\":\"[selected-result] Two-sided Fisher exact test on 16 chamber deployments: odds ratio 21.00 for ebullition on intact versus drained peat (7/8 against 2/8 deployments), p = 0.0406.\"}]}],\"counterevidence_atoms\":[{\"description\":\"The report is transparent about the repeated-visit structure: it states that every collar was visited on more than one survey week and provides a per-collar deployment table, so the dependence is disclosed rather than hidden. Disclosure, however, does not make the Fisher exact test's independence assumption hold.\",\"observed_value\":\"Per-collar table listing 2-3 deployments per collar\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":5,\"end_line\":7,\"quoted_text\":\"Each row is one static-chamber deployment scored for a visible ebullition\\n(bubble release) event during the closure window. Deployments were made on\\npermanent collars, and every collar was visited on more than one survey week.\"},{\"path\":\"results/report.md\",\"start_line\":13,\"end_line\":18,\"quoted_text\":\"| FEN-01 | intact | 3 | 3 |\\n| FEN-02 | intact | 3 | 2 |\\n| FEN-03 | intact | 2 | 2 |\\n| DRN-01 | drained | 3 | 1 |\\n| DRN-02 | drained | 3 | 0 |\\n| DRN-03 | drained | 2 | 1 |\"}]},{\"description\":\"The data description itself states 'One trial is: one row', which could be read as sanctioning row-level trials. But the same description names collar_id as the independent-unit column, and a Fisher exact test additionally requires trials to be independent, which repeated closures on the same permanent collar are not.\",\"observed_value\":\"One trial is: one row\",\"source_spans\":[{\"path\":\"data-description.md\",\"start_line\":27,\"end_line\":28,\"quoted_text\":\"Independent unit column: collar_id\\nOne trial is: one row\"}]}],\"falsification_attempt\":\"I tried to defeat the finding two ways. (1) Alternative reading that rows are legitimately independent: the description explicitly designates collar_id as the independent-unit column, states the same collar appears in several rows, and drainage_status is fixed per collar block, so repeated rows from one collar are repeated measurements of one unit within one treatment cell; the row-independence assumption of Fisher's exact test cannot be defended from the supplied files. (2) Robustness check at the declared unit level: with only 3 intact and 3 drained collars, the most extreme possible collar-level 2x2 table ([[3,0],[0,3]]) has a two-sided Fisher exact p of C(3,3)*C(3,0)/C(6,3)*2 = 2/20 = 0.10, so no collar-level dichotomization of these data can reach p < 0.05. The reported p = 0.0406 is therefore attributable to counting 16 dependent rows as trials rather than to information available at the 6 independent units. I also verified the arithmetic pipeline: the CSV yields 7/1 intact and 2/6 drained row-level counts, matching the report's table, odds ratio (7*6)/(1*2) = 21.00, and 16 total rows, confirming the selected result flows from the row-level pooling in split_counts.\",\"cross_case_evidence_used\":false,\"unresolved_material_questions\":[],\"self_reported_confidence\":\"high\"}]}","structured_output":{"reviewer_participant_id":"actor:dependence-free-batch-j2-reviewer-fable-46","reviews":[{"case_id":"case:56e4106c5ef7a44d29c8","verdict":"demonstrated_issue","issue_class":"issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure","bounded_statement":"Within the supplied files for case:56e4106c5ef7a44d29c8, the selected result (two-sided Fisher exact test on 16 chamber deployments, odds ratio 21.00, p = 0.0406) is produced by entering all 16 deployment rows into a 2x2 Fisher exact test as independent trials, even though the data description designates collar_id as the independent unit and each of the 6 collars contributes 2-3 repeated deployment rows. The Fisher exact test assumes each counted trial is independent, so the repeated entries from the same collar inflate the effective sample size from 6 collars to 16 rows; a collar-level 3-vs-3 comparison cannot reach p < 0.05 under any outcome (minimum two-sided Fisher p = 0.1 for complete separation), so the reported significance depends entirely on the repeated per-collar entries.","root_cause":"The workflow's split_counts function pools every CSV row by drainage_status into the 2x2 contingency table without any aggregation to the collar_id level, and compose() feeds those pooled row counts directly into scipy.stats.fisher_exact. Because drainage_status is a fixed property of each collar and each collar appears in 2-3 rows (repeated survey weeks), the same independent unit enters the row-independent Fisher exact procedure multiple times, which the report then explicitly frames as 16 independent trials.","evidence_atoms":[{"description":"The data description declares collar_id as the independent-unit column while a row is a single chamber closure on a collar in one survey week, establishing that multiple rows per collar are repeated measurements of one unit.","observed_value":"Independent unit column: collar_id; one row = one closure on one collar in one survey week","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":26,"end_line":28,"quoted_text":"One row is: one static-chamber closure on one permanent collar in one survey week\nIndependent unit column: collar_id\nOne trial is: one row"}]},{"description":"The data description states that each collar was revisited across survey weeks 24, 27 and 31, so every collar contributes 2 or 3 rows, and that the same collar appears in several rows of the file.","observed_value":"6 collars, each with 2-3 deployments (16 rows total)","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":7,"end_line":9,"quoted_text":"Each collar was revisited in survey weeks 24, 27 and 31; two collars were not\nreachable in week 31, so those collars contribute two deployments instead of\nthree."},{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":14,"end_line":16,"quoted_text":"- `collar_id`: the permanent collar the chamber was seated on (FEN-* collars\n  are intact, DRN-* collars are drained). The same collar appears in several\n  rows of the file."}]},{"description":"drainage_status, the grouping factor of the 2x2 test, is a fixed property of the collar's block rather than of the individual visit, so repeated rows from one collar always re-enter the same treatment cell.","observed_value":"drainage_status constant within collar","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":17,"end_line":18,"quoted_text":"- `drainage_status`: `intact` or `drained`, a property of the collar's block\n  rather than of the individual visit."}]},{"description":"The input data confirm repeated rows per collar: e.g. FEN-01 appears in rows D001, D007 and D013 (weeks 24, 27, 31), and DRN-01 appears in rows D004, D010 and D015.","observed_value":"FEN-01: 3 rows; DRN-01: 3 rows; 16 rows across 6 collars","source_spans":[{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":2,"end_line":2,"quoted_text":"D001,FEN-01,intact,24,13.8,-3.5,yes"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":8,"end_line":8,"quoted_text":"D007,FEN-01,intact,27,17.4,-5.8,yes"},{"path":"data/input.csv","start_line":14,"end_line":14,"quoted_text":"D013,FEN-01,intact,31,14.6,-4.7,yes"}]},{"description":"The workflow pools all rows (not collars) into the 2x2 table via split_counts and submits the row-level counts directly to scipy.stats.fisher_exact, with no per-collar aggregation feeding the test (per_collar is used only for the descriptive table).","observed_value":"fisher_exact([[hit_i, miss_i], [hit_d, miss_d]]) on row-level counts 7/1 vs 2/6","source_spans":[{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":21,"end_line":24,"quoted_text":"def split_counts(rows, status):\n    subset = [row for row in rows if row[\"drainage_status\"] == status]\n    hits = sum(1 for row in subset if row[\"ebullition_detected\"] == DETECTED)\n    return hits, len(subset) - hits"},{"path":"workflow/analysis.py","start_line":42,"end_line":48,"quoted_text":"hit_i, miss_i = split_counts(rows, \"intact\")\n    hit_d, miss_d = split_counts(rows, \"drained\")\n    n_i = hit_i + miss_i\n    n_d = hit_d + miss_d\n    n_total = n_i + n_d\n    _, p_value = fisher_exact([[hit_i, miss_i], [hit_d, miss_d]],\n                              alternative=\"two-sided\")"}]},{"description":"The report explicitly asserts that every deployment row entered the Fisher test as one independent trial and that the test counts 16 rows produced by only 6 collars, which is the repeated entry of independent units into a row-independent procedure.","observed_value":"16 rows treated as independent trials from 6 collars","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":22,"end_line":24,"quoted_text":"The 16 deployments were cross-tabulated by drainage status and ebullition\noutcome, and the 2x2 table was submitted to a two-sided Fisher exact test.\nEvery deployment row entered the table as one independent trial."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":38,"end_line":39,"quoted_text":"Deployments were treated as independent trials, so the test counts 16 rows\nacross the 6 collars that produced them."}]},{"description":"The selected statistical conclusion rests on the row-level test: p = 0.0406 is below 0.05 only because n = 16 deployments rather than 6 collars.","observed_value":"p = 0.0406, odds ratio 21.00, 7/8 vs 2/8 deployments","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":36,"end_line":36,"quoted_text":"[selected-result] Two-sided Fisher exact test on 16 chamber deployments: odds ratio 21.00 for ebullition on intact versus drained peat (7/8 against 2/8 deployments), p = 0.0406."}]}],"counterevidence_atoms":[{"description":"The report is transparent about the repeated-visit structure: it states that every collar was visited on more than one survey week and provides a per-collar deployment table, so the dependence is disclosed rather than hidden. Disclosure, however, does not make the Fisher exact test's independence assumption hold.","observed_value":"Per-collar table listing 2-3 deployments per collar","source_spans":[{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":5,"end_line":7,"quoted_text":"Each row is one static-chamber deployment scored for a visible ebullition\n(bubble release) event during the closure window. Deployments were made on\npermanent collars, and every collar was visited on more than one survey week."},{"path":"results/report.md","start_line":13,"end_line":18,"quoted_text":"| FEN-01 | intact | 3 | 3 |\n| FEN-02 | intact | 3 | 2 |\n| FEN-03 | intact | 2 | 2 |\n| DRN-01 | drained | 3 | 1 |\n| DRN-02 | drained | 3 | 0 |\n| DRN-03 | drained | 2 | 1 |"}]},{"description":"The data description itself states 'One trial is: one row', which could be read as sanctioning row-level trials. But the same description names collar_id as the independent-unit column, and a Fisher exact test additionally requires trials to be independent, which repeated closures on the same permanent collar are not.","observed_value":"One trial is: one row","source_spans":[{"path":"data-description.md","start_line":27,"end_line":28,"quoted_text":"Independent unit column: collar_id\nOne trial is: one row"}]}],"falsification_attempt":"I tried to defeat the finding two ways. (1) Alternative reading that rows are legitimately independent: the description explicitly designates collar_id as the independent-unit column, states the same collar appears in several rows, and drainage_status is fixed per collar block, so repeated rows from one collar are repeated measurements of one unit within one treatment cell; the row-independence assumption of Fisher's exact test cannot be defended from the supplied files. (2) Robustness check at the declared unit level: with only 3 intact and 3 drained collars, the most extreme possible collar-level 2x2 table ([[3,0],[0,3]]) has a two-sided Fisher exact p of C(3,3)*C(3,0)/C(6,3)*2 = 2/20 = 0.10, so no collar-level dichotomization of these data can reach p < 0.05. The reported p = 0.0406 is therefore attributable to counting 16 dependent rows as trials rather than to information available at the 6 independent units. I also verified the arithmetic pipeline: the CSV yields 7/1 intact and 2/6 drained row-level counts, matching the report's table, odds ratio (7*6)/(1*2) = 21.00, and 16 total rows, confirming the selected result flows from the row-level pooling in split_counts.","cross_case_evidence_used":false,"unresolved_material_questions":[],"self_reported_confidence":"high"}]},"ttft_ms":32497,"ttft_stream_ms":4948,"time_to_request_ms":11,"type":"result","duration_ms":77186,"uuid":"7e7c5f6c-6857-47b5-9c16-9701fb5fa21a"}
